\f[I]TYPE\f[R]] [\f[B]\[en]overwrite\f[R]] [\f[B]\[en]no\-color\f[R]]
[\f[B]\[en]help\f[R]] [\f[B]\[en]version\f[R]] [\f[I]MODULE\f[R]\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]minios\-image\-compose\f[R] generates a MiniOS ISO image and
optionally adds specified SquashFS modules.
The tool detects the bootloader type (SYSLINUX, GRUB, or mixed) and
supports customizable menu types with localization support.
.PP
Input identities, modes, mtimes, and SHA256 digests are recorded before
the build.
Before \f[B]xorriso\f[R] starts, the backend attempts to bind mutable
regular graft inputs to private, metadata\-preserving reflinks.
When a reflink is unavailable, the original path remains the graft
source and strict identity and digest checks before and after
\f[B]xorriso\f[R] detect mutation.
Hardlinks are not used.
Read\-only mounted inputs may remain directly bound.
The destination free\-space preflight reserves the estimated ISO size
and additional headroom.
Graft points are passed to \f[B]xorriso\f[R] as separate arguments, and
the completed image is checked for its filesystem tree, volume label,
BIOS boot record, UEFI boot record, and system area.
Rock Ridge modes and symbolic links are preserved in no\-customization
builds without chmod or writes against the source.
.PP
Positional modules must be readable, non\-symlink regular files with
portable \f[B].sb\f[R] basenames and valid SquashFS superblocks.
Names beginning with two digits and a hyphen are placed at the MiniOS
top level; other added modules are placed below
\f[B]minios/modules/\f[R].
Destination collisions and duplicate case\-folded basenames are rejected
across source top\-level modules, \f[B]modules/\f[R], positional
modules, image overlays, and session captures because the runtime
flattens those layers by basename.
.PP
ISO preparation, writing, verification, and publication run with the
caller\[cq]s EUID.
For optional session capture, a non\-root invocation runs only trusted
\f[B]/usr/bin/savechanges\f[R] through trusted
\f[B]/usr/bin/pkexec\f[R]; it does not elevate
\f[B]minios\-image\-compose\f[R], \f[B]xorriso\f[R], or later phases.
A private cancellation marker in the mode 0700 build directory lets the
unprivileged parent request root\-side process\-group cleanup even when
direct signals fail with EPERM.
Callers should invoke \f[B]minios\-image\-compose\f[R] normally rather
than prefixing the complete command with \f[B]pkexec\f[R].
.PP
Image customization is limited to boot configuration, boot artwork, and
one declarative filesystem overlay.
It never chroots, installs packages, executes project scripts, or
modifies the source tree.
Effective boot configs and overlay files are copied into private storage
through no\-follow descriptors before tools consume them.
Only the optional running\-session capture can cross the pkexec
boundary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 4
\f[B]\-e\f[R], \f[B]\[en]exclude\f[R] \f[I]REGEX\f[R]
Exclude source paths matching the POSIX extended regular expression
\f[I]REGEX\f[R].
Newlines and invalid expressions are rejected.
An exclusion may not remove required boot files, kernel/initramfs files,
core or kernel modules, the selected menu, the configuration, or the
requested manifest.
.TP 4
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\[en]name\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Publish to this exact output pathname.
The default is minios\-YYYYMMDD_HHMM.iso in the current directory.
The image is built in a private mode 0700 directory on the destination
filesystem and atomically published through retained directory
descriptors.
An existing pathname is refused unless \f[B]\[en]overwrite\f[R] is
present.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]source\f[R] \f[I]MINIOS_DIR\f[R]
Use the canonical MiniOS content root containing \f[B]boot/\f[R] and
MiniOS module files.
Modules may be top\-level or below \f[B]modules/\f[R].
The source directory itself must not be a symbolic link and is never
modified.
Without this option, the current livekit/dracut source discovery is
used.
Session capture compares the effective source\-module fingerprint with
the running mounted base.
If reliable module binding is unavailable, an explicit source is
accepted only when it is the discovered running source.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]config\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Graft the readable, non\-symlink regular file as
\f[B]minios/config.conf\f[R].
The default is \f[B]/etc/live/config.conf\f[R].
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]volume\-label\f[R] \f[I]LABEL\f[R]
Set a 1 to 32 character printable ASCII ISO volume label.
The default is \f[B]MINIOS\f[R].
Labels outside the strict ISO 9660 uppercase, digit, and underscore set
are accepted by xorriso with a warning.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]manifest\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Validate a readable, non\-symlink JSON object and graft it as
\f[B]minios/build\-manifest.json\f[R].
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]capture\-changes\f[R] \f[I]MODE\f[R]
Capture the running writable session with the savechanges
\f[B]exact\f[R], \f[B]clean\f[R], or \f[B]selected\f[R] profile.
The module is assigned the first safe numeric order after every included
source and additional module, then grafted as
\f[B]minios/ORDER\-session\-changes.sb\f[R].
Orders through 999998 are supported; collisions and an exhausted range
are rejected.
Capture metadata binds the module to the running boot ID and mounted
base\-module fingerprint.
\f[B]clean\f[R] is a narrow path\-based privacy reduction, not proof
that no allowed software file contains a secret.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]capture\-selection\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Use this readable, non\-symlink strict JSON selection with
\f[B]\[en]capture\-changes selected\f[R].
It is required for selected capture and forbidden for exact or clean
capture.
The selection is copied to private build storage before it is hashed or
consumed.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]capture\-compression\f[R] \f[I]TYPE\f[R]
Compress the captured module with \f[B]zstd\f[R], \f[B]gzip\f[R],
\f[B]lzo\f[R], or \f[B]xz\f[R].
The default is \f[B]zstd\f[R].
This option is invalid without \f[B]\[en]capture\-changes\f[R].
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]boot\-timeout\f[R] \f[I]SECONDS\f[R]
Set the timeout in every authoritative GRUB and native SYSLINUX
configuration reached from the active bootloader roots.
The value is an integer from 0 through 300.
GRUB receives seconds and SYSLINUX receives deciseconds.
Without this option, source timeout directives are preserved.
In a multilingual layout, both the language menu and each reachable
session menu are updated.
The minimal MiniOS SYSLINUX\-to\-GRUB chainloader remains unchanged
because GRUB is authoritative after its fixed immediate handoff.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]default\-boot\f[R] \f[I]MODE\f[R]
Select \f[B]resume\f[R], \f[B]new\f[R], \f[B]choose\f[R],
\f[B]fresh\f[R], or \f[B]toram\f[R] as the default MiniOS session.
GRUB entries are identified by the MiniOS semantic classes
\f[B]resume\f[R], \f[B]new\f[R], \f[B]switch\f[R], \f[B]live\f[R], and
\f[B]ram\f[R], with kernel session arguments used as a consistency check
or fallback.
Native SYSLINUX entries are identified from \f[B]APPEND\f[R] session
arguments and checked against known \f[B]LABEL\f[R] values.
Translated \f[B]MENU LABEL\f[R] and GRUB title text are never used.
Every authoritative reachable session menu must prove the requested
mapping or the build fails.
A multilingual first\-stage language menu keeps its language default;
its reachable \f[B]main.cfg\f[R] or \f[CR]lang/*.cfg\f[R] session menus
are changed instead.
The exact MiniOS \f[CR]LINUX /minios/boot/grub/i386\-pc/lnxboot.img\f[R]
and \f[CR]INITRD /minios/boot/grub/i386\-pc/core.img\f[R] chain
delegates session semantics to the reachable GRUB graph; malformed or
mixed chainloader/native layouts are rejected.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]kernel\-args\f[R] \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
Append \f[I]TEXT\f[R] unchanged to every actual GRUB \f[B]linux\f[R],
\f[B]linuxefi\f[R], or \f[B]linux16\f[R] command and every SYSLINUX
kernel stanza \f[B]APPEND\f[R] line in the effective config graph.
The UTF\-8 encoding must be 1 to 4096 bytes, printable, use ordinary
spaces only, and avoid quoting, expansion, comment, redirection,
grouping, wildcard, and command\-separator characters that cannot have
identical GRUB and SYSLINUX meaning.
The text is never evaluated or passed through shell substitution.
Logs and \f[B]image\-customization.json\f[R] contain only its UTF\-8
byte count and SHA256.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]boot\-background\f[R] \f[I]PNG\f[R]
Replace \f[B]minios/boot/bootlogo791.png\f[R] and existing source
\f[CR]bootlogo*.png\f[R] variants with a private snapshot of
\f[I]PNG\f[R].
The input must be a readable non\-symlink regular file no larger than 16
MiB and 1 through 8192 pixels on each axis.
Validation checks bounded chunks, CRCs, one leading IHDR, a
GRUB/SYSLINUX\-compatible non\-interlaced RGB, indexed, or RGBA format,
palette ordering, consecutive IDAT chunks, the zlib stream and scanline
filters, one final IEND, and absence of trailing data.
The source is not modified.
Every embedded target is extracted and compared with the recorded digest
after ISO creation.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]overlay\-directory\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R]
Package one real, readable directory, given as an absolute path outside
the MiniOS source, the output, and the work directory, as a rootless
\f[B]ORDER\-image\-overlay.sb\f[R] module.
The overlay is resolved independently of the current working directory,
so the frontend may run \f[B]minios\-image\-compose\f[R] from a private
job directory.
Safe directories, regular files, empty directories, executable bits,
mtimes, and relative symlinks that remain inside the overlay root are
supported.
Device nodes, sockets, FIFOs, filesystem crossings, control characters
in names, absolute links, and escaping links are rejected.
Privilege bits are cleared and module ownership is normalized with
\f[B]mksquashfs \-all\-root\f[R].
The tree is copied through retained no\-follow descriptors, rescanned
for mutation, and only the private copy is passed to mksquashfs.
The generated and ISO\-extracted SquashFS images are each unpacked
privately and compared against the captured path, type, mode,
symlink\-target, regular\-size, and SHA256 manifest.
Missing, extra, or changed objects fail publication.
The dynamic order follows every source and additional module and
precedes a session\-capture module when both are requested.
.TP 4
\f[B]\[en]menu\f[R] \f[I]TYPE\f[R]
Set menu type for both GRUB and SYSLINUX.
\f[I]TYPE\f[R] can be: \f[B]multilang\f[R] (default) \- menu with
language selection \f[I]LANG\f[R] \- specific language code (en_US,
ru_RU, de_DE, es_ES, it_IT, id_ID, pt_BR, pt_PT, fr_FR)
