.SH BOOTLOADER SUPPORT
The tool automatically detects and supports three bootloader
configurations:
.TP 4
\f[B]syslinux\-grub\f[R] (most common)
Uses the exact minimal MiniOS SYSLINUX \f[B]lnxboot.img/core.img\f[R]
stanza to hand legacy BIOS execution to GRUB; GRUB is also used directly
for UEFI.
Detection requires both chainloader images, and customization validates
the stanza before treating the reachable GRUB graph as authoritative.
.TP 4
\f[B]grub\-only\f[R]
Uses GRUB directly for all boot scenarios.
Detected when only GRUB BIOS components are present.
.TP 4
\f[B]syslinux\-native\f[R]
Uses SYSLINUX natively without GRUB components.
Detected when only SYSLINUX files are present.
.PP
The bootloader type is automatically detected based on the boot files
present in the source MiniOS system.
.SH MENU TYPES
.TP 4
\f[B]multilang\f[R] (default)
Multi\-language menu with language selection screen.
.TP 4
\f[I]Language codes\f[R]
Fully localized menus with translated text and language\-specific
themes.
Supported languages: en_US (English), ru_RU (Russian), de_DE (German),
es_ES (Spanish), it_IT (Italian), id_ID (Indonesian), pt_BR (Portuguese
Brazil), pt_PT (Portuguese Portugal), fr_FR (French).
.SH EXAMPLES
Create basic ISO from current live system:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose
.EE
.PP
Create ISO with custom name:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-name my_custom_minios.iso
.EE
.PP
Create an ISO from an explicit source and configuration:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-source /media/minios \-\-config ./config.conf \-\-name ./custom.iso
.EE
.PP
Include a GUI\-generated build manifest and custom label:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-manifest ./build.json \-\-volume\-label \[aq]MINIOS LAB\[aq] \-\-name ./custom.iso
.EE
.PP
Create an ISO using the narrow software\-only session policy:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-capture\-changes clean \-\-name ./software\-session.iso
.EE
.PP
Create an image with a five\-second session menu, a fresh\-session
default, and additional safe kernel arguments:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-boot\-timeout 5 \-\-default\-boot fresh \[rs]
    \-\-kernel\-args \[aq]audit=1 mitigations=auto\[aq] \-\-name ./custom\-boot.iso
.EE
.PP
Add a project filesystem overlay and boot artwork without changing the
source tree:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-overlay\-directory \[dq]$PWD/image\-overlay\[dq] \[rs]
    \-\-boot\-background ./art/boot.png \-\-name ./branded.iso
.EE
.PP
Capture only paths chosen by a frontend:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-capture\-changes selected \[rs]
    \-\-capture\-selection ./session\-selection.json \-\-name ./selected.iso
.EE
.PP
Exclude heavy applications:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-exclude \[aq]firefox|libreoffice|gimp\[aq] \-\-name minios_lite.iso
.EE
.PP
Create minimal text\-mode ISO:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-exclude \[aq]desktop|xorg|apps\[aq] \-\-name minios_minimal.iso
.EE
.PP
Add extra modules to current system:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose development_tools.sb games.sb \-\-name minios_extended.iso
.EE
.PP
Create localized Russian ISO:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-menu ru_RU \-\-name minios_ru.iso
.EE
.PP
Combine exclusions with additions:
.IP
.EX
minios\-image\-compose \-\-exclude \[aq]games\[aq] extra_productivity.sb \-\-name minios_work.iso
.EE
.SH FILES
Without \f[B]\[en]source\f[R], the script discovers MiniOS loaded from
live media or RAM.
With explicit \f[B]\[en]source\f[R] and \f[B]\[en]config\f[R] paths,
source discovery is not required.
Session capture still requires a running MiniOS session whose mounted
base\-module fingerprint matches the selected source.
Required system tools are resolved from
\f[B]/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin\f[R], independently of the
caller\[cq]s PATH; \f[B]mkfs.ext2\f[R] is provided by
\f[B]e2fsprogs\f[R].
Default boot files are located in:
.TP 4
\f[I]/run/initramfs/memory/data/minios/boot/\f[R]
Boot files location when running from live system
.TP 4
\f[I]/lib/live/mount/data/minios/boot/\f[R]
Boot files location with dracut\-based live media
.TP 4
\f[I]/etc/live/config.conf\f[R]
Default configuration input
.SH OUTPUT PROTOCOL
Human\-readable messages retain the stable \f[B]I:\f[R], \f[B]W:\f[R],
and \f[B]E:\f[R] prefixes.
Major phases are additionally emitted as untranslated identifiers:
\f[B]P:prepare\f[R], \f[B]P:customize\f[R] before customization work,
\f[B]P:capture\f[R] when enabled, \f[B]P:boot\-copy\f[R],
\f[B]P:persistence\f[R], \f[B]P:iso\-write\f[R], \f[B]P:verify\f[R],
\f[B]P:complete\f[R], and \f[B]P:cancelled\f[R] during privileged
cancellation.
The nested \f[B]savechanges\f[R] phases \f[B]P:capture\-inventory\f[R],
\f[B]P:capture\-copy\f[R], \f[B]P:capture\-compress\f[R],
\f[B]P:capture\-complete\f[R], and \f[B]P:cancelled\f[R] pass through
unchanged.
Frontends should consume these identifiers rather than translated prose.
.PP
Capture logs state the profile, union backend, boot ID, source and
base\-module fingerprints, dynamic order and ISO target, nonzero module
size, module SHA256, and selection SHA256 when applicable.
Selection include and exclude paths are never logged; invalid or
unmatched selections report only generic classes or counts.
\f[B]minios/session\-capture.json\f[R] embeds the same digest\-only
schema\-3 report without changing the GUI\-authored
\f[B]build\-manifest.json\f[R].
.PP
After structural ISO checks, \f[B]minios\-image\-compose\f[R] extracts
the embedded capture module and report, plus the build manifest when
present.
It rehashes their actual bytes, validates the report against privileged
capture metadata and expected order, performs full SquashFS checks on
the extracted module, and rejects any mismatch before atomic
publication.
.PP
When image customization is enabled,
\f[B]minios/image\-customization.json\f[R] is embedded with product kind
\f[B]minios\-image\-customization\f[R] and schema version 1.
It records transformed config targets and digests, timeout and semantic
default values, kernel\-argument byte count and SHA256, background
dimensions/targets/digest, and image\-overlay target, order, size,
SHA256, entry count, and input\-tree fingerprint.
It contains no kernel argument text or host input path.
Verification rejects duplicate fields, non\-finite numbers, unknown
fields, incorrect types, and incorrect optional\-section presence.
The expected report is reconstructed independently from retained CLI
intent, the byte\-bound boot plan and its source digests, retained
target\-set identities, the original background and overlay identities,
and bytes extracted from the ISO.
It does not trust or compare against the mutable local report.
Every customized config transformation is recomputed, every extracted
background is parsed and hashed, and the generated and embedded overlay
modules must match the original retained module size and SHA256.
Both overlay modules are then checked against the captured filesystem
manifest.
.PP
Boot transformation intentionally supports direct GRUB
\f[B]configfile\f[R] or \f[B]source\f[R] references and direct native
SYSLINUX \f[B]CONFIG\f[R] or \f[B]INCLUDE\f[R] references whose
normalized targets remain below \f[B]minios/boot/\f[R], plus the exact
MiniOS SYSLINUX\-GRUB chainloader described above.
Dynamic references, shell\-generated config names, GRUB submenus,
SYSLINUX nested menus, commented or multiline kernel commands, ambiguous
session arguments, missing semantic entries, mixed chainloader/native
layouts, and config graph cycles are rejected rather than guessed.
Existing native SYSLINUX \f[B]MENU DEFAULT\f[R] directives are removed
and \f[B]ONTIMEOUT\f[R] is aligned with the requested semantic default.
Source layouts using unsupported forms require normalization before
customization; builds without boot customization continue to preserve
them unchanged.
.PP
Private reflink snapshots close pathname and ancestor replacement races
for mutable xorriso inputs when the filesystem supports reflinks.
Otherwise, the original graft remains identity\-bound and any metadata
or content change detected around xorriso fails the build.
Read\-only mounted sources are treated as immutable.
Every supervised external command must also finish its process group; a
leader that exits while descendants remain causes bounded TERM/KILL
cleanup and build failure.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
\f[B]0\f[R]
Successful completion.
.TP 4
\f[B]1\f[R]
General, validation, tool, destination, write, or verification error.
.TP 4
\f[B]2\f[R]
Invalid source tree, required modules, menu, kernel/initramfs, or boot
files.
.TP 4
\f[B]3\f[R]
Invalid, unsafe, colliding, unreadable, or non\-SquashFS additional
module.
.TP 4
\f[B]4\f[R]
Cannot create or prepare secure temporary build data.
.TP 4
\f[B]130\f[R]
Interrupted by INT or TERM.
Rootless overlay mksquashfs and its process group are terminated
directly.
During privileged capture, the marker is created atomically before
direct signal attempts, then the parent waits a bounded interval for
savechanges to terminate, reap, and clean its root process group.
Private temporary output is removed through retained descriptors.
